{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RiDycbJWgNpysqs3gPYG2c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CW2vzHsh4XdSGj5rmBTcx1","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus: **methods and experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Credit Assignment Granularity in Multi-Turn Agent RL: An Empirical Taxonomy of Failure Modes\"\n\nThe paper studies credit assignment granularity in multi-turn agent RL on ALFWorld, comparing:\n- Step-independent GRPO (fixed-length windows of h steps)\n- Episode-level GRPO (full trajectory as single sample)\n- GAE-based training (per-token temporal-difference advantages)\n\nThree main findings:\n1. Step-independent training has a stochastic performance cliff at small h (seed-dependent collapse)\n2. Episode-level GRPO plateaus at 23.8%, outperformed by GAE(λ=0.95) at 43.8% (+20pp)\n3. Selective verification (B-IAVR) trained on episode-level agent fails completely (Pareto-dominated by naive baselines including random)\n\n## Key Tables from the Paper\n\n**Table 1: h-sweep (3 seeds, 15 steps)**\nh=2: 1.0% mean, 1.8pp std, 67% collapse\nh=5: 11.5% mean, 10.4pp std, 33% collapse  \nh=10: 14.6% mean, 6.3pp std, 0% collapse\nh=20: 19.8% mean, 4.8pp std, 0% collapse\nfull: 25.0% mean, 3.1pp std, 0% collapse\n\n**Table 2: Episode-level GRPO (5 seeds)**\nMean at step 15: 23.8% ± 6.2pp. All plateau by step 10-15. Step 50: 25.0%.\n\n**Table 3: GAE comparison**\nEpisode GRPO: 23.8%, GAE(λ=1.0): 37.5%, GAE(λ=0.95): 43.8% (+20pp)\n\n**Table 4: Sign misattribution analysis**\nSign-outcome correlation: 0.72 (predicted >0.95)\nSign-local quality correlation: 0.28 (predicted <0.1)\nMixed-sign episodes: 18% (predicted 0%)\nγ^k decay R²: 0.45 (predicted >0.8)\n\n**Table 5: B-IAVR results**\nVerification rate: 88% (target: ≤60%), cost reduction: 12% (target: ≥40%), success Δ: -6.2pp\n\n**Table 6: Verification strategy comparison**\nB-IAVR (21.9%, 88% verify, 0.92x cost) dominated by Random p=0.5 (23.4%, 50%, 0.67x), Confidence (25.0%, 62%, 0.74x), and even No-verify has best success/token ratio.\n\n**Table 7: Reward signal ablation**\nAction-change: 21.9%/88% (worst, Pareto-dominated). Random trigger: 23.4%/52%. Confidence: 25.0%/65%.\n\n**Table 8: ScienceWorld transfer**\nEpisode-level: 15.0%, Step-independent h=5: 12.0% (3pp, p=0.38, not significant)\n\n**Table 9: 7B scaling**\nEpisode-level: 35%, Step-independent h=5: 22% (gap narrows from 28pp to 13pp)\n\n**Table 10: Per-task breakdown**\nSuccess correlates with task length (r=0.45). Step-independent fails on tasks >25 steps.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe review_context reveals that:\n- The h-sweep cliff is NOT robust: at h=5, std=10.4pp means cliff is a random variable, not structural\n- Episode-level GRPO plateaus and is massively outperformed by GAE (+20pp), meaning per-token credit IS necessary\n- Action-change reward has essentially zero correlation with success after controlling for entropy (partial corr=0.03, p=0.42)\n- Causal intervention shows verification corrections ≈ random perturbations (3pp, p=0.31, d=0.12)\n- B-IAVR is Pareto-dominated by ALL baselines including random\n- ScienceWorld transfer is not significant (p=0.38)\n- 7B scaling shows the \"catastrophic failure\" is specific to small models\n\n## Your Review Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Experimental rigor**: seed counts, statistical significance, evaluation set size (only 32 tasks!), CI widths, reproducibility\n2. **Methodological soundness**: Are the comparisons fair? Are baselines appropriate? Is the GAE implementation comparable?\n3. **Claim-evidence alignment**: Do the claims match what the data actually shows? Are conclusions overdrawn or appropriately hedged?\n4. **Missing experiments or controls**: What's missing?\n5. **Absolute performance levels**: Best method (GAE) reaches only 43.8% on ALFWorld where prior work reports 72-97% — what does this mean?\n\nOutput format:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Bulleted list with specifics]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Bulleted list with specifics, most critical first]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing Experiments / Controls\n[Bulleted list]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Bulleted list]\n\n### Preliminary Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":32481,"cache_read_input_tokens":24216,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":32481},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cbfaf1eb-86b3-4c62-a538-5d4d55066586","uuid":"031906cf-94cf-4eca-ba83-36dceeb8cc6b"}
