{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01F1pTqSTuJkWsdBXFGmV2uB","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_1/projects/cost-verify-agent-rl/literature_map.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# B-IAVR Literature Map\\\\n# Updated: 2026-05-17\\\\n# Categories: direct-competitor, method-component, background, evaluation\\\\n# Roles: predecessor, competitor, baseline, evaluation, background\\\\n\\\\npapers:\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # Direction 1: Self-Verification / Self-Correction in LLM Agents\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n  - id: kamoi2024self-correction-survey\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"When Can LLMs Actually Correct Their Own Mistakes? A Critical Survey of Self-Correction of LLMs\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Kamoi et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2406.01297\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Foundational survey establishing that pure intrinsic self-correction is unreliable.\\\\n      Validates B-IAVR's use of grounded verification (environment re-observation) over prompted self-feedback.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: tie2025correctbench\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Can LLMs Correct Themselves? A Benchmark of Self-Correction in LLMs\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Tie et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2025 Datasets & Benchmarks\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2510.16062\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Benchmark showing self-correction has diminishing returns when applied uniformly.\\\\n      Supports B-IAVR's selective verification design over blanket correction.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: self-verification-dilemma-2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Self-Verification Dilemma: Experience-Driven Suppression of Overused Checking in LLM Reasoning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Long et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2602.03485\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Empirically validates that >90% of self-verification is confirmatory, ~2% corrective.\\\\n      Directly supports B-IAVR's core thesis that verification should be selective.\\\\n      Their experience-pool approach is complementary to B-IAVR's action-change signal.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: zhang2025incentivize-self-verify\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Incentivizing LLMs to Self-Verify Their Answers\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Zhang et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2506.01369\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Unifies answer generation and verification via single RL training process.\\\\n      Parallel to B-IAVR: they train verification into generation; B-IAVR trains when to trigger separate verification.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: mar2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"MAR: Multi-Agent Reflexion Improves Reasoning Abilities in LLMs\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Ozer et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2512.20845\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Multi-agent self-correction with diverse critic personas.\\\\n      Complementary: MAR improves correction quality; B-IAVR decides when to trigger correction.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: ssr2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"SSR: Socratic Self-Refine for Large Language Model Reasoning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Shi et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2511.10621\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Structured Socratic questioning for self-refinement.\\\\n      Complementary: improves correction step quality; B-IAVR focuses on when to invoke correction.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: verifiagent2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"VerifiAgent: a Unified Verification Agent in Language Model Reasoning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Han et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"EMNLP 2025 Findings\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2504.00406\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Unified verification agent with meta-verification + tool-based adaptive verification.\\\\n      Focuses on how to verify (tool selection); B-IAVR focuses on when to verify (policy learning).\\\\n      Complementary: VerifiAgent's verification quality could enhance B-IAVR's verification step.\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # Direction 2: Agent RL Training\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n\\\\n  - id: feng2025gigpo\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"GiGPO: Group-in-Group Policy Optimization for LLM Agent Training\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Feng et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2505.10978\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: predecessor\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Core training algorithm for B-IAVR. Nested group structure provides step-level credit\\\\n      assignment without learned critic. >12% over GRPO on ALFWorld, >9% on WebShop.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: selfguide2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Co-Evolution of Policy and Internal Reward for Language Agents (Self-Guide)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Wang et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2604.03098\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: predecessor\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Direct predecessor. B-IAVR extends Self-Guide's co-evolving internal reward.\\\\n      The self-guidance signal is the foundation for action-change detection triggering verification.\\\\n      8% improvement over env-reward-only on ALFWorld with Qwen3-1.7B/4B.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: spear2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Learn the Ropes, Then Trust the Wins: Self-imitation with Progressive Exploration for Agentic Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Qin et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2509.22601\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Curriculum-based self-imitation for sparse-reward agent tasks.\\\\n      Up to +16.1% on ALFWorld over GRPO. Self-imitation curriculum could augment B-IAVR training.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: wei2025webagent-r1\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"WebAgent-R1: Training Web Agents via End-to-End Multi-Turn RL\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Wei et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"EMNLP 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2505.16421\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Extends GRPO to multi-turn (M-GRPO) with dynamic context compression.\\\\n      Qwen-2.5-3B: 6.1% to 33.9% on WebArena-Lite. M-GRPO multi-turn extension relevant to B-IAVR.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: dapo2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"DAPO: An Open-Source LLM Reinforcement Learning System at Scale\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Yu et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2503.14476\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      RL training stabilization techniques (Clip-Higher, Dynamic Sampling).\\\\n      Applicable to B-IAVR's RL training to prevent entropy collapse.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: digiRL2024\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"DigiRL: Training In-The-Wild Device-Control Agents with Autonomous RL\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Bai et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2406.11896\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Online RL for device control agents. Demonstrates online RL viability for interactive agents.\\\\n      49.5% absolute improvement over SFT. Parallel approach to B-IAVR in text-based setting.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: li2025sorl\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"SORL: Stabilizing Off-Policy RL for Long-Horizon Agent Training\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Li et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2511.20718\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Turn-level importance sampling + clipping-triggered normalization for stable multi-turn agent RL.\\\\n      Directly applicable to B-IAVR's multi-turn ALFWorld training stability.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: agentic-rl-survey-2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"The Landscape of Agentic Reinforcement Learning for LLMs: A Survey\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Zhang et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"TMLR 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2509.02547\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Comprehensive survey formalizing agentic RL. Contextualizes B-IAVR as training agents\\\\n      to make meta-decisions (when to verify) via RL within the POMDP framework.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: skillmaster2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"SkillMaster: Toward Autonomous Skill Mastery in LLM Agents\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Yang et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2605.08693\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      DualAdv-GRPO: separately estimates advantages for task-solving and skill-editing decisions.\\\\n      +8.8% on ALFWorld, +9.3% on WebShop. DualAdv structure could inform B-IAVR's\\\\n      separation of action and verification advantages.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: t2po2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"T2PO: Uncertainty-Guided Exploration Control for Stable Multi-Turn Agentic RL\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Wang et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2026 Spotlight\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2605.02178\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Token- and turn-level uncertainty monitoring for stable multi-turn agent RL.\\\\n      Triggers thinking intervention when marginal uncertainty change drops below threshold.\\\\n      Evaluated on ALFWorld and WebShop. Conceptually related: uncertainty-based trigger vs\\\\n      B-IAVR's action-change trigger, but T2PO targets training stability, not deployment verification.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: gvpo2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"GVPO: Group Verification-based Policy Optimization for Interactive Coding Agents\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Dai et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    arxiv_id: null  # no arXiv preprint; OpenReview ID: RY47Tq0VsV\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Advantage shaping with outcome-verifiable + process-verifiable signals.\\\\n      +12.7% over o1 on AppWorld. Integrates intermediate execution feedback as step-level\\\\n      verification signals. Different focus: uses verification for credit assignment during\\\\n      training; B-IAVR learns when to invoke verification at deployment.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: agentv-rl2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"AgentV-RL: Scaling Reward Modeling with Agentic Verifier\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Zhang et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2604.16004\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      Bidirectional forward/backward agentic verifier for reward modeling in RL.\\\\n      4B model surpasses SOTA ORMs by 25.2%. Addresses verification quality in reward modeling;\\\\n      B-IAVR addresses verification timing in agent deployment. Complementary.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: pa-moe2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"PA-MoE: Phase-Aware Mixture of Experts for Agentic Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Li et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2602.17038\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: evaluation\\\\n    role: baseline\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      [NEW-0516] Lightweight phase router learns latent phase boundaries from RL objective.\\\\n      PA-MoE+GiGPO with Qwen2.5-1.5B achieves 93.8% on ALFWorld (+7.7 over GiGPO 86.1%).\\\\n      1.5B outperforms vanilla 7B GiGPO (90.2%). New small-model SOTA on ALFWorld.\\\\n      Orthogonal architecture innovation; important baseline to cite.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: sdar2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"SDAR: Self-Distilled Agentic Reinforcement Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Lu et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2605.15155\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      [NEW-0516] On-policy self-distillation as gated auxiliary objective during RL training.\\\\n      Token-level gate decides supervision intensity. +9.4% over GRPO on ALFWorld.\\\\n      Complementary: SDAR improves RL training; B-IAVR learns verification policy.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: hcapo2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"HCAPO: Hindsight Credit Assignment for Long-Horizon LLM Agents\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Tan et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2603.08754\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      [NEW-0516] LLM-as-critic refines step-level Q-values via hindsight reasoning +\\\\n      multi-scale advantage for critical decision states.\\\\n      +13.8% on ALFWorld, +7.7% on WebShop over GRPO (Qwen2.5-7B).\\\\n      Could improve B-IAVR's reward signal for verification decision steps.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: act2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"ACT: Agentic Critical Training\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Liu et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2603.08706\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    relevance_note: >\\\\n      [NEW-0516] Trains agents to judge action quality via critical comparison reward.\\\\n      +5.07 over IL, +4.62 over RL. Different axis: ACT improves reflection quality;\\\\n      B-IAVR decides when to reflect.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: rwml2026\\\\n    ti\\\\n\\\\n... [截断: 省略 17932 字符，传 max_chars=0 获取完整内容]\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 29932, \\\"truncated\\\": true}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01CW2vzHsh4XdSGj5rmBTcx1","session_id":"cbfaf1eb-86b3-4c62-a538-5d4d55066586","uuid":"38ddad8a-8f34-4527-8d0d-f0d808fea771","timestamp":"2026-05-17T14:16:26.929Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RiDycbJWgNpysqs3gPYG2c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UNFTeGEtuUhE3jgQp6XwEg","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus: **novelty, positioning, and related work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Credit Assignment Granularity in Multi-Turn Agent RL: An Empirical Taxonomy of Failure Modes\"\n\nThe paper studies credit assignment granularity in multi-turn agent RL on ALFWorld, comparing step-independent GRPO, episode-level GRPO, and GAE. Framed as an \"empirical taxonomy of failure modes.\" Three contributions claimed:\n1. Multi-seed characterization of step-independent performance cliff (stochastic, seed-dependent)\n2. Mechanistic evidence for advantage sign misattribution (exists but weaker than predicted)\n3. Negative result showing selective verification fails at <30% base agent competence\nPlus cross-environment (ScienceWorld) and cross-scale (7B) experiments.\n\n## Paper Structure (9 pages)\n- Abstract: 150+ words, frames as empirical study of credit assignment granularity\n- Introduction: Sets up contrarian hypothesis (\"coarser might suffice\"), then immediately concedes it's false\n- Section 2 (Related Work): Multi-turn agent RL, credit assignment in LLM RL, self-verification\n- Section 3 (Method): Problem setting, three credit assignment variants (step-independent, episode-level, GAE), selective verification (B-IAVR), hypothesized failure mechanisms\n- Section 4 (Experiments): h-sweep, multi-seed, GAE comparison, sign misattribution verification, valid action bonus, selective verification (6 subsections), transfer & scaling\n- Section 5 (Discussion): Three paragraphs\n- Section 6 (Conclusion): One paragraph\n- Limitations: One paragraph\n\n## Key Results\n- Step-independent h-sweep: stochastic cliff, h=5 has 10.4pp std across 3 seeds\n- Episode-level GRPO: 23.8% mean, plateaus, massively outperformed by GAE (43.8%)\n- Sign misattribution: exists but weak (0.72 vs predicted >0.95 correlation)\n- B-IAVR: complete failure, Pareto-dominated by random baselines\n- ScienceWorld: 3pp advantage, not significant (p=0.38)\n- 7B: gap narrows from 28pp to 13pp\n\n## Related Work Mentioned in Paper\n- RAGEN: step-independent GRPO for multi-turn agents\n- ArCher: hierarchical RL for multi-turn agents\n- HiPER: 97.4% on ALFWorld at 7B\n- GAGPO: 93.5% on ALFWorld at 1.5B\n- Turn-PPO: turn-level value functions\n- MT-GRPO: turn-level rewards for QA\n- iStar: implicit process reward with dual advantages\n- Zhang et al. survey: 47 credit assignment methods\n- Self-Guide: always-on verification, 72.7% ALFWorld at 1.7B\n- ARES: supervised verification router, 52.7% token reduction\n- ReflAct: prompting-based reflection with GPT-4\n\n## Important Context\nThe paper's original framing was \"coarser credit suffices\" (contrarian positioning). The data directly refutes this:\n- GAE >> episode-level GRPO by 20pp\n- The community consensus (finer credit is better) is correct\n- The paper pivoted to \"empirical taxonomy of failure modes\"\n\nThe best result in this paper (GAE at 43.8%) is far below SOTA:\n- HiPER: 97.4% ALFWorld (7B)\n- GAGPO: 93.5% ALFWorld (1.5B)\n- Self-Guide: 72.7% ALFWorld (1.7B)\n\n## Your Review Task\nEvaluate:\n\n1. **Novelty**: Is \"coarser credit fails\" novel? The community already pursues finer credit. Does confirming the consensus add value? Is \"taxonomy of failure modes\" a sufficient intellectual contribution?\n2. **Positioning**: The paper starts with a contrarian hypothesis then immediately admits it's wrong. Does this framing work? Is it honest academic practice or does it read like a pivot?\n3. **Related work completeness**: Are key references missing? Is the positioning against prior work accurate?\n4. **Contribution significance**: Would a top venue accept: (a) confirming that GAE > episode-level GRPO, (b) characterizing a stochastic cliff with 3 seeds, (c) a negative result on selective verification where the base agent is too weak to meaningfully test the idea?\n5. **Narrative coherence**: Does the paper tell a coherent story or does it read like a collection of negative results repackaged?\n6. **Comparison to SOTA**: The paper's best is 43.8% vs 72-97% in prior work at same or smaller scale. Does this undermine the findings?\n\nOutput format:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Bulleted list with specifics]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Bulleted list with specifics, most critical first]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Assessment of Contribution Significance\n[1-2 paragraphs]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Bulleted list]\n\n### Preliminary Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":32481,"cache_read_input_tokens":24216,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":32481},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cbfaf1eb-86b3-4c62-a538-5d4d55066586","uuid":"b2a4942b-093a-4871-87a2-c0ed92af9e19"}
